#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa1b2c-464f-5ae1-8216-6860374fb383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377014} {:answer/id #uuid "2aef1cad-2085-56eb-96cc-582c5dfdb6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377015} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8da38d-2a9a-5822-80a5-2b33c6eeabf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377016} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b7040e-060d-5de5-aa54-ed36a74842c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377017} {:answer/id #uuid "f51c3771-886d-54be-a41d-f564302fb129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51c3771-886d-54be-a41d-f564302fb129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377018} :answer-field/id #uuid "568fdcad-55b5-582b-bda8-3b5896f1b121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04d9df30-194a-5086-89b7-3926acc05406" :question/math-academy-id "q-140526" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd33f0f-ac59-5bb7-8eab-8fc3304b7dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131853} {:answer/id #uuid "bf549025-65c8-59ad-b011-27bcdaeb3d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131854} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b17f01-5b9b-5e82-ad6f-af25fecc3359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131855} {:answer/id #uuid "d90b88fa-9237-535e-82e7-10795f845c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131856} {:answer/id #uuid "1033738b-90a4-52bd-beff-1f934ab746c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d90b88fa-9237-535e-82e7-10795f845c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131856} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23c3527-b6d4-5e7e-9ea5-7f1b9c99c2f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84b52cd1-ef6d-51a0-aeac-25f2437c64c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40739" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ $b=0,$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|+|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc94d1b-c3bb-5d39-bdd4-7d9c5f4492be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132015} {:answer/id #uuid "9608785c-1c34-54d5-949a-ac3050665960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132016} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd830c-2962-5fdd-9868-4fd32c1a8173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132017} {:answer/id #uuid "10e2fa35-7ae2-531e-899b-64fb1dc00efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132018} {:answer/id #uuid "5b05fdd3-f47b-5220-8b02-f07c74ddf2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd830c-2962-5fdd-9868-4fd32c1a8173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132017} :answer-field/id #uuid "69cd7137-218b-50d5-bfc6-37eba31e5527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4388fdfa-5eb8-5165-a253-2f6080a33d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41035" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $c=-4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5308b4f-3612-5103-9346-5aeb29ca39f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377021} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc6f8a0-0f05-5037-99d5-ce080dabb761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377022} {:answer/id #uuid "5c888d00-5c35-539c-a93f-9a5fafb946cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023} {:answer/id #uuid "ffadd238-d345-5c9b-8a56-6fb9959abf66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024} {:answer/id #uuid "25b6112e-d9c7-5330-ad5f-ab6f7c733076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffadd238-d345-5c9b-8a56-6fb9959abf66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4accb12-60f8-595c-9266-8a13f681fc3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36c3eb21-9ecf-5719-be65-00aec9bbb34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-86850" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{(-\\frac{13}{3})}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5496c6-b4f8-574f-9f9b-b9eeb45a2b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5d44d7-4b60-5339-b10f-ef4df2293796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377029} {:answer/id #uuid "00df12d0-5301-5e79-bb0e-92e3d1cce05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030} {:answer/id #uuid "fde3a84f-bed4-5b48-b38b-ccf645358a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377031} {:answer/id #uuid "9376245a-11f6-5724-9939-b6cae9ef5c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9376245a-11f6-5724-9939-b6cae9ef5c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5fd301-39f3-5c20-9d23-dacc79cc7677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14893e66-75a9-5088-abbc-ee6dea1a7da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86854" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},$ $c=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|+|\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$"}]}
